    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "No perceived Doppler shift occurs if and only if the observed frequency f' equals the source frequency f.",
      "evidence": "By definition, a Doppler shift is a change in frequency; 'no shift' implies f' = f.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The condition f' = f is satisfied when the source and detector move with the same velocity vector relative to the medium.",
      "evidence": "If v_d = v_s (same magnitude and direction), the ratio (v + v_d)/(v + v_s) becomes 1, resulting in f' = f. This corresponds to zero relative velocity between the two.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Option (C) allows for the condition where both source and detector travel in the same direction with equal speeds.",
      "evidence": "The option states they travel in the same direction; if their speeds are equal, the condition in Step 3 is met, resulting in no shift.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Options (A), (B), and (D) necessarily result in a non-zero Doppler shift.",
      "evidence": "In (A) and (B), one object is stationary while the other moves, so v_d ≠ v_s. In (D), they move in opposite directions, so their velocity vectors cannot be equal (unless both are zero, which contradicts 'moving away'). Thus, f' ≠ f in all these cases.",
